## 8. Token consumption

```css
/* app.css — import order for a Tailwind v4 project */
@import "tailwindcss";
@import "@poodle64/design-tokens/tokens.tw.css"; /* @theme block */
@import "@poodle64/design-tokens/tokens.css";    /* :root + .dark */
@import "@poodle64/ui/styles.css";               /* shadcn surface + its registration */

/* @poodle64/ui ships in node_modules, outside Tailwind's default src/ scan. */
@source "../node_modules/@poodle64/ui/dist";

/* Root font-size: 100% keeps the browser/OS default (nominally 16px) while
   still scaling with a reader's own changed browser text-size preference.
   Never a fixed px value here -- that would silently override that
   preference instead of honouring it. */
html {
  font-size: var(--ds-font-size-base);
}

/* Per-app primary override — one of the only two changes from shared
   defaults. Skip this block entirely if you adopt a catalogued palette
   below: a palette already sets primary, its foreground and ring. */
:root {
  --ds-color-primary: oklch(0.50 0.155 XXX);
  --ds-color-primary-foreground: oklch(0.99 0.005 XXX);
  --ds-color-ring: oklch(0.50 0.155 XXX);
}
.dark {
  --ds-color-primary: oklch(0.70 0.140 XXX);
  --ds-color-primary-foreground: oklch(0.20 0.030 XXX);
  --ds-color-ring: oklch(0.70 0.140 XXX);
}
```

**Or adopt a catalogued palette instead**, which is the other sanctioned
change and the easier one: it carries an accent AND a surface tone, and every
palette in the catalogue is gated for WCAG AA contrast in both modes, which a
hand-picked accent is not until someone checks it.

Pick one or the other, and mean it. A palette block is
`:root[data-ds-palette='…']`, which outranks a plain `:root { --ds-color-primary: … }`
on specificity — so an app that adopts a palette and ALSO leaves an old primary
override in `app.css` keeps a block that now does nothing, with no error, no
lint and no failing test to say so. Delete the override when you adopt a
palette; a dead declaration someone later "fixes" by changing its value is
worse than no declaration at all.

```css
@import "@poodle64/design-tokens/palettes.css"; /* after tokens.css */
```

```html
<html data-ds-palette="parchment"></html>
```

The twenty palettes and what each is for are in
`@poodle64/design-tokens`'s README. Pick one by eye at
`?surface=palette` in `packages/ui/harness`, which renders the whole component
set under it. Nothing else in the app changes, and no component knows.

**Do not hand-write a shadcn alias layer.** Every app used to declare
`--card` / `--popover` / `--muted` / `--accent` / `--secondary` / `--input` /
`--radius` and the `-foreground` pairs itself. That half-works in a way nothing
catches: declaring them as plain custom properties makes the variable exist but
never tells Tailwind v4 they are theme colours, so `bg-card`, `bg-muted`,
`bg-accent` and `border-input` compile to no CSS rule at all — no build error,
no lint hit, no failing test, just classes in the DOM with nothing behind them
(design-system#3). Since `@poodle64/ui@2026.7.2` the import above ships that
whole surface, mapping and registration together, and each name aliases a
`--ds-*` token, so the primary override reaches all of it.

The one part still owned per app is sidebar and chart colours: this package
ships no sidebar or chart component, and chrome hue is genuinely an app's own
decision, so an app that needs them declares and registers them itself.

TypeScript:
```ts
import { DS_COLOR_PRIMARY_LIGHT } from "@poodle64/design-tokens";
```
